  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A carved wooden face survived 12,000 years in a bog. Workers hauled it out of Russian peat in 1890, in ten pieces.",
        "visual_query": "ural mountains peat bog",
        "visual_fallback": "dark peat ground"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Larch wood, nine feet tall, cut from a tree already 159 years old. Faces stare out of it at several heights, geometric lines running between them.",
        "visual_query": "siberian carved wooden idol",
        "visual_fallback": "wood carving closeup"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Nothing else from that era is like it. So who was carving portraits before anyone farmed, built a city, or fired a pot?",
        "visual_query": "siberian taiga larch forest",
        "visual_fallback": "larch forest"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The answer is the bog. Peat sealed the larch from oxygen, so it never rotted. Hunter-gatherers made monumental art; we just never had the evidence.",
        "visual_query": "russian peat bog excavation",
        "visual_fallback": "bog digging"
      },
      {
        "narration": "In 2021, new radiocarbon dates put the carving at about 12,000 years old. Older than Stonehenge by seven thousand years.",
        "visual_query": "ural forest misty dawn",
        "visual_fallback": "misty forest"
      }
    ],
